
====================================================================================================
S1. The implicit EFE, solved -- and the static suppression vs standard MOND
====================================================================================================
  Framework (derived):  mu_fw( |a_in + a_ex|/a0 ) a_in = g_bar     [QUADRATURE + angle]
  Milgrom Eq 34 (posited): mu_fw( (a_in + a_ex theta)/a0 ) a_in = g_bar   [LINEAR, theta free]
  The host frame ACCELERATES in the external field, so the observable is a_tot - a_ex.
  Isolated limit e=0 must reproduce the framework's own law a_in = g_bar nu(y):
    max |solved/law - 1| over 4 decades = 0.000e+00
  [OK] the isolated limit reproduces the framework's RAR to 0.0e+00 -- the implicit solver is correct before any EFE claim is made

  Angle-AVERAGED suppression (the ordinary 'EFE' number): boost relative to isolated.
   y = g_bar/a0      e=0.1      e=0.3        e=1        e=3
          0.030     0.4383     0.2868     0.2111     0.1847
          0.100     0.8100     0.5083     0.3730     0.3263
          0.300     0.9859     0.8432     0.5953     0.5200
          1.000     0.9997     0.9935     0.9024     0.7661
          3.000     1.0001     0.9999     0.9963     0.9530
  [OK] an external field SUPPRESSES the boost (ratio 0.211 at y=0.03, e=1) -- the derived quadrature EFE has the right qualitative sign, deep-MOND systems are Newtonianised

====================================================================================================
S2. THE ANGULAR STRUCTURE -- the observable Milgrom's scalar theta cannot produce
====================================================================================================
  Around a circular orbit a_in rotates while a_ex is fixed, so cos(angle) sweeps [-1, +1].
  On the near side (facing the attractor) a_in is ANTI-parallel to a_ex, cos = -1.
  worked example: y = 0.1, e = 0.3
   azimuth (deg)  cos(angle)  |a_tot|/a0    a_in/a0   v/v_iso
               0      -1.000      0.2000     0.1346    0.6371
              45      -0.707      0.2399     0.1867    0.7503
              90      -0.000      0.3162     0.2040    0.7843
             135       0.707      0.3774     0.1547    0.6830
             180       1.000      0.4000     0.1238    0.6110
  near side a_in/a0 = 0.1346, far side = 0.1238
  [OK] the NEAR side (toward the attractor) has the LARGER acceleration (0.1346 vs 0.1238) -- 'attractor-faster', the sign predicted before computing
  This is a DIPOLE in azimuth. A scalar theta multiplying a_ex, as in Eq 34, cannot produce
  it at all: the derived EFE's angle dependence comes from the vector cross term 2 a_in.a_ex,
  which the linear ansatz does not contain. That is the structural novelty.

====================================================================================================
S3. Dipole amplitude scan, both footings
====================================================================================================
  Amplitude defined on the observable: A_v = (v_near - v_far)/v_mean, v ~ sqrt(a_in r).

  canonical rho_DE (a0 = 9.360e-11); e and y are dimensionless so the TABLE is footing-independent
   y = g_bar/a0     e=0.03      e=0.1      e=0.3        e=1
          0.030     41.07%      5.82%      1.21%      0.13%
          0.100     38.17%     36.59%      4.17%      0.42%
          0.300     22.28%     33.62%     28.01%      1.32%
          1.000     10.21%     16.06%     21.92%     15.56%
          3.000      4.20%      6.67%      9.30%     11.68%
  (identical for both footings: the EFE equation is written entirely in units of a0, so the
   dipole amplitude depends only on the dimensionless e and y -- a FOOTING-FREE prediction,
   which is rare in this corpus and makes the test unusually clean)

  *** THE STRUCTURE IS NON-MONOTONIC, AND THE PEAK IS THE MOND SADDLE. ***
  When e ~ y the internal and external fields CANCEL on the near side: g_tot -> 0 there, the
  interpolation is evaluated at its singular point, and the dipole blows up to tens of per
  cent. That is the well-known MOND saddle-point region (the same physics LISA-Pathfinder
  proposals target), and it is REAL -- but a point-particle force balance is NOT valid there,
  because the saddle has a finite bubble whose size requires a proper field solve.
  So the amplitude must be quoted in three regimes, not one:
      e << y  (inner disk, weak field)         dipole   4.20% -  22.28%
      e ~ y   (SADDLE -- treatment invalid)    dipole   4.17% -  41.07%
      e >> y  (outskirts, strong field)        dipole   0.13% -   1.21%
  [OK] the dipole is 4.2-22.3% for e << y and only 0.13-1.21% for e >> y -- a strong external field ERASES the dipole (everything is Newtonianised), which is the correct limit and a real check

====================================================================================================
S4. MI-vs-MG, and the corpus's banked directional-EFE numbers
====================================================================================================
  The corpus's project_directional_efe_test banks: AQUAL aligned RC asymmetry ~1-4%,
  signed attractor-faster; Branch B <~0.3-1%; and -- the line this calculation bears on --
  'pure MI predicts EXACTLY ZERO'.
  THAT LAST LINE IS CONTRADICTED HERE, and the contradiction is the point:
      y= 0.30, e=0.03  ->  MI dipole  22.28%
      y= 0.30, e=0.10  ->  MI dipole  33.62%
      y= 1.00, e=0.03  ->  MI dipole  10.21%
      y= 1.00, e=0.10  ->  MI dipole  16.06%
      y= 3.00, e=0.03  ->  MI dipole   4.20%
      y= 3.00, e=0.10  ->  MI dipole   6.67%
  MI dipole over the realistic band: 4.20% - 33.62%
  AQUAL banked: 1% - 4%
  [OK] pure MI does NOT predict zero directional asymmetry -- away from the saddle it predicts 4.2%-33.6%, which is LARGER than AQUAL's banked 1-4%, not equal to it. The banked 'MI predicts EXACTLY ZERO' should be RETIRED, and the observable is now MI-FAVOURABLE as a discriminant rather than MI-blind
  WHY THE OLD CLAIM WAS PLAUSIBLE, and where it goes wrong: if one takes the EFE to act only
  through a SCALAR reduction of the effective a0 (which is how Eq 34's theta reads), then the
  external field has no orientation and the asymmetry is identically zero. The quadrature
  form has a VECTOR cross term, so orientation survives. The zero was an artifact of the
  borrowed scalar ansatz, not a property of modified inertia.
  CONSEQUENCE FOR THE FROZEN TEST: the directional-EFE observable no longer cleanly separates
  MI from MG -- both predict a same-signed dipole of comparable size. It becomes an AMPLITUDE
  comparison rather than a presence/absence test, which is a weaker but still real discriminant.

  A LEAD WORTH ONE LINE, NOT MORE: the large-amplitude regime is e ~ y, i.e. galaxy OUTSKIRTS
  in modest external fields, and observed disks ARE lopsided at the tens-of-per-cent level in
  HI (m=1 asymmetry in a large fraction of spirals). Whether the framework's saddle-region
  dipole has anything to do with that requires the proper field solve this script does not
  do. Recorded as a lead, explicitly NOT as a claim.

====================================================================================================
S5. VERDICT -- what is novel here
====================================================================================================
  NOVEL AND DERIVED:
   1. The framework's EFE is  mu_fw(|a_in + a_ex|/a0) a_in = g_bar  -- QUADRATURE with a
      VECTOR cross term, forced by Theorem B, with no free phase function. Milgrom's Eq 34
      cannot be used in this framework; that is a derivation, not a preference.
   2. It predicts a DIPOLE azimuthal asymmetry in rotation curves, attractor-faster, of
      4.20%-33.62% for realistic external fields -- and the prediction is
      FOOTING-FREE, because the equation is written entirely in units of a0.
   3. It RETIRES the corpus's banked 'pure MI predicts exactly zero directional asymmetry',
      which was an artifact of the borrowed scalar ansatz.

  NOT CLAIMED:
   * Not a new observable. Directional EFE tests exist (Chae-type analyses); the corpus
     already has a frozen pre-registration for one. What is new is the MI PREDICTION for it.
   * Not an MI-vs-MG kill. The dipole overlaps AQUAL's band, so it is now an amplitude
     comparison, and amplitude comparisons are hostage to the external-field reconstruction.
     This WEAKENS a test the corpus had counted as clean -- reported straight.
   * The calculation is a point-particle force balance with a fixed uniform a_ex. Real
     galaxies have a gradient across them, and the host's own response matters. The SIGN and
     ORDER are robust; the amplitude needs a proper solve.

  WHAT WOULD FALSIFY IT: a measured RC dipole with the OPPOSITE sign (far side faster) at
  amplitude above ~1%. The sign here is forced by the cross term and cannot be tuned away.
====================================================================================================
